WEBVTT

00:00:02.430 --> 00:00:03.120
CHET HUSK: Hey, folks.

00:00:03.120 --> 00:00:06.000
Welcome to Build, and especially
welcome to this session

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:09.660
on simplifying.NET
installs with dotnetup.

00:00:09.660 --> 00:00:11.250
I'm Chet Husk, and
I've been working

00:00:11.250 --> 00:00:14.100
on the.NET SDK for
a few years now.

00:00:14.100 --> 00:00:17.220
And I'm excited to show you this
new product we've been working

00:00:17.220 --> 00:00:21.210
on and show you how it might
help tackle some problems

00:00:21.210 --> 00:00:26.730
that you have been facing
managing.NET itself.

00:00:26.730 --> 00:00:28.290
Before we really
get into things,

00:00:28.290 --> 00:00:31.770
I want to take a few minutes to
let you know what we're going

00:00:31.770 --> 00:00:32.970
to be looking at today.

00:00:32.970 --> 00:00:37.020
So first, I want to shape
the problem for you

00:00:37.020 --> 00:00:41.810
and let you see how I see
getting.NET on your machine

00:00:41.810 --> 00:00:44.270
and managing versions over time.

00:00:44.270 --> 00:00:46.790
And then I want to briefly
sketch out the outline

00:00:46.790 --> 00:00:49.980
of our plan on how we'd
like to solve this.

00:00:49.980 --> 00:00:53.250
And then we'll get right
into the meat of some demos

00:00:53.250 --> 00:00:58.920
of what's coming in our initial
preview versions of dotnetup.

00:00:58.920 --> 00:01:01.470
Finally, we'll come back,
and we'll talk about what's

00:01:01.470 --> 00:01:04.680
on the roadmap for dotnetup
and when you can expect to see

00:01:04.680 --> 00:01:07.290
and use it and get
hands on yourself.

00:01:07.290 --> 00:01:09.270
Okay. Are you ready?

00:01:09.270 --> 00:01:12.210
Let's take a look at what
we're dealing with here.

00:01:12.210 --> 00:01:16.500
So if you install.NET
today, we think you fall

00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:18.930
into basically one
of two buckets.

00:01:18.930 --> 00:01:21.960
You are either using
Visual Studio on Windows,

00:01:21.960 --> 00:01:26.100
where Visual Studio manages the
toolchain for you and updates

00:01:26.100 --> 00:01:29.820
on a frequent cadence and brings
you all of those feature band

00:01:29.820 --> 00:01:33.240
and servicing bug fixes
that you know and love,

00:01:33.240 --> 00:01:36.300
or you're everyone else.

00:01:36.300 --> 00:01:40.080
And that's exactly the problem
that we'd like to solve.

00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:44.760
If you look at installing.NET
on different platforms,

00:01:44.760 --> 00:01:50.130
it's a very, shall we say,
heterogeneous situation.

00:01:50.130 --> 00:01:52.650
Here's a short list of a
bunch of the different ways

00:01:52.650 --> 00:01:56.130
that you can install.NET today.

00:01:56.130 --> 00:01:58.320
If you break these
into categories,

00:01:58.320 --> 00:02:02.310
what you have is
IDE-based distribution,

00:02:02.310 --> 00:02:04.560
a package-manager-based
distribution,

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:10.050
and then you have manual
distribution of various kinds.

00:02:10.050 --> 00:02:13.260
For IDE-based
distributions on Windows,

00:02:13.260 --> 00:02:15.510
you have primarily
Visual Studio,

00:02:15.510 --> 00:02:18.480
and then across platform,
you have Visual Studio Code,

00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:23.070
where my team owns a Visual
Studio Code extension whose job

00:02:23.070 --> 00:02:26.610
is to get you the SDK
versions that your projects

00:02:26.610 --> 00:02:28.890
and tools need to run.

00:02:28.890 --> 00:02:32.820
Outside of the IDEs, you
have package managers,

00:02:32.820 --> 00:02:37.470
things like WinGet or
Homebrew on macOS or Linux,

00:02:37.470 --> 00:02:39.900
and we'll get there
in just a second.

00:02:39.900 --> 00:02:43.290
And these tools use the
system's capabilities

00:02:43.290 --> 00:02:45.900
to acquire.NET packages.

00:02:45.900 --> 00:02:49.380
These are really nice
for a number of reasons,

00:02:49.380 --> 00:02:52.110
the most interesting
of which is the kind

00:02:52.110 --> 00:02:55.230
of centralized
management you get.

00:02:55.230 --> 00:02:58.530
But for some people, that
model is not a good fit,

00:02:58.530 --> 00:03:03.930
and so they reach for more
manual ways of installing macOS.

00:03:03.930 --> 00:03:08.220
Some of the most common of these
include the actual.NET website,

00:03:08.220 --> 00:03:11.160
which is get.dot.net,
which is one

00:03:11.160 --> 00:03:13.440
of my favorite things to say.

00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:16.890
We have a set of install
scripts for PowerShell

00:03:16.890 --> 00:03:21.420
and various POSIX shells
that people often use

00:03:21.420 --> 00:03:25.530
to manage their CLI
and SDK installations.

00:03:25.530 --> 00:03:29.340
And then there's a whole
ecosystem of tools

00:03:29.340 --> 00:03:32.910
that exist specifically
to manage versions

00:03:32.910 --> 00:03:36.300
of various software
development toolchains.

00:03:36.300 --> 00:03:39.390
For.NET, there's a community
project named DNVM

00:03:39.390 --> 00:03:41.520
or.NET Version Manager.

00:03:41.520 --> 00:03:46.410
But there are also more
extensible or pluggable systems

00:03:46.410 --> 00:03:50.370
for this, like
Mise-en-Place or ASDF.

00:03:50.370 --> 00:03:53.130
There are too many to count
here, but these are some

00:03:53.130 --> 00:03:57.180
of the most familiar or
most frequently used.

00:03:57.180 --> 00:03:59.400
I haven't talked about
Linux yet, though.

00:03:59.400 --> 00:04:02.430
But I do want to point out that
all of the mechanisms here

00:04:02.430 --> 00:04:06.300
on this slide that you see with
an asterisk are not mechanisms

00:04:06.300 --> 00:04:09.000
that are owned and
operated by Microsoft.

00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:12.720
These are community mechanisms
in a bunch of different ways.

00:04:12.720 --> 00:04:16.020
And sometimes they use
packages under the hood.

00:04:16.020 --> 00:04:19.560
And sometimes they use
just zips or tarballs.

00:04:19.560 --> 00:04:22.410
And in some cases, like
Homebrew, they can use both,

00:04:22.410 --> 00:04:24.540
depending on your preferences.

00:04:24.540 --> 00:04:29.400
So already you can see it's
a very mixed bag of ways

00:04:29.400 --> 00:04:34.350
to get.NET and the pros and
cons of each mechanism.

00:04:34.350 --> 00:04:38.700
And we're about to get even more
complex as we enter the world

00:04:38.700 --> 00:04:41.730
of Linux distributions.

00:04:41.730 --> 00:04:43.380
You have a bunch
of different ways:

00:04:43.380 --> 00:04:46.500
those same three categories
of package managers,

00:04:46.500 --> 00:04:48.210
the dotnet-install scripts,

00:04:48.210 --> 00:04:53.790
and then the manual
installation tools.

00:04:53.790 --> 00:04:57.510
But this is coupled
with the fact that,

00:04:57.510 --> 00:05:00.720
for the package managers,
there's been this long arc

00:05:00.720 --> 00:05:04.080
of history where sometimes
Microsoft provided packages

00:05:04.080 --> 00:05:05.670
for your package manager,

00:05:05.670 --> 00:05:09.030
and sometimes your
distro provided packages

00:05:09.030 --> 00:05:10.650
for your package manager.

00:05:10.650 --> 00:05:12.780
And sometimes you had both.

00:05:12.780 --> 00:05:15.840
So there's a long list of
decision trees that you have

00:05:15.840 --> 00:05:19.260
to go through just to
install our software.

00:05:19.260 --> 00:05:23.400
You have to decide if you're
okay with certain restrictions

00:05:23.400 --> 00:05:26.880
on the feature bands that you
will have available to you

00:05:26.880 --> 00:05:30.210
because the.NET SDK
ships four times a year.

00:05:30.210 --> 00:05:34.380
And each quarterly shipment
contains net new features

00:05:34.380 --> 00:05:36.220
and enhancements.

00:05:37.470 --> 00:05:41.130
So do you want those, or do you
want the centralized update

00:05:41.130 --> 00:05:45.150
mechanism that the distros'
package manager give you?

00:05:45.150 --> 00:05:48.960
Do you need different versions
across your different repos?

00:05:48.960 --> 00:05:53.310
If so, the package managers may
not be a good fit for that.

00:05:53.310 --> 00:05:57.630
So it's pretty easy to, as you
start looking at the needs

00:05:57.630 --> 00:06:01.290
of your individual repos or
your individual developers,

00:06:01.290 --> 00:06:02.880
that you find that
the happy path

00:06:02.880 --> 00:06:04.770
of package managers
doesn't work for you.

00:06:04.770 --> 00:06:06.540
And you start having to reach

00:06:06.540 --> 00:06:12.210
for these local management
solutions like dotnet-install

00:06:12.210 --> 00:06:17.820
or DNVM, Mise-en-Place,
etc. These tools are great.

00:06:17.820 --> 00:06:21.390
They do the job, but in the
case of the install scripts,

00:06:21.390 --> 00:06:26.370
there's no central management so
you can keep track of what SDKs

00:06:26.370 --> 00:06:29.730
and toolchains are installed
where on your system.

00:06:29.730 --> 00:06:34.260
There's no way to easily update
individual installations or all

00:06:34.260 --> 00:06:36.540
of the installations
on your system.

00:06:36.540 --> 00:06:41.550
And in addition, often these
local mechanisms tend to clash

00:06:41.550 --> 00:06:44.760
with any global or
system-level installs.

00:06:44.760 --> 00:06:47.880
And all of these
statements are true even

00:06:47.880 --> 00:06:51.210
on other platforms than Linux.

00:06:51.210 --> 00:06:55.620
So there's this very complex
installation problem

00:06:55.620 --> 00:06:57.540
that users have to solve.

00:06:57.540 --> 00:07:01.890
And then when you guys find
bugs or gaps in our software,

00:07:01.890 --> 00:07:04.470
and you want us to consider
making enhancements

00:07:04.470 --> 00:07:07.620
or fixing those bugs,
it's difficult on our end

00:07:07.620 --> 00:07:11.340
to categorize how that
installation occurred

00:07:11.340 --> 00:07:14.070
and validate that that
installation is correct

00:07:14.070 --> 00:07:15.690
in the first place.

00:07:15.690 --> 00:07:17.370
So there's pain on both ends.

00:07:17.370 --> 00:07:20.040
Believe me on that.

00:07:20.040 --> 00:07:23.400
So what do we, as
a tooling team,

00:07:23.400 --> 00:07:25.860
want to do to solve
this big problem?

00:07:25.860 --> 00:07:27.870
Well, there's all those
different installation

00:07:27.870 --> 00:07:31.380
mechanisms, and we discovered
we would make another one.

00:07:31.380 --> 00:07:33.570
And this is the one that
will solve all the problems.

00:07:33.570 --> 00:07:35.580
And I'm only half joking here.

00:07:35.580 --> 00:07:38.040
We'll take a look in a little
bit more detail in a moment

00:07:38.040 --> 00:07:42.600
and see how we plan on tackling
some of these problems.

00:07:42.600 --> 00:07:45.720
But I could not resist
the opportunity to call

00:07:45.720 --> 00:07:48.510
out to Randall
Munroe's comic here.

00:07:48.510 --> 00:07:54.960
Our plan for solving this
platform-specific and managed

00:07:54.960 --> 00:07:59.010
versus unmanaged split
is to make a tool

00:07:59.010 --> 00:08:02.190
that behaves the same
on every platform.

00:08:02.190 --> 00:08:07.470
And that leans into
global installations

00:08:07.470 --> 00:08:11.700
that do not require
user elevation.

00:08:11.700 --> 00:08:13.920
So in this case,
when we say global,

00:08:13.920 --> 00:08:15.960
we don't mean system global.

00:08:15.960 --> 00:08:19.290
We mean user global,
installations of.NET

00:08:19.290 --> 00:08:22.500
that are scoped to your user
directory or a location

00:08:22.500 --> 00:08:25.860
where you specifically manage

00:08:25.860 --> 00:08:28.920
and that don't impact
others on your system.

00:08:28.920 --> 00:08:31.800
So we want to make this
tool and make it fast,

00:08:31.800 --> 00:08:36.270
make it lightweight, use
native AOT to make it execute

00:08:36.270 --> 00:08:39.090
as quickly as possible
and get out of your way.

00:08:39.090 --> 00:08:42.360
We want it to leverage
the existing mechanisms

00:08:42.360 --> 00:08:48.180
that users use to describe
what SDKs and tools you want.

00:08:48.180 --> 00:08:50.100
Today, that's global JSON.

00:08:50.100 --> 00:08:55.230
But the hope is that with a
shared way of interacting

00:08:55.230 --> 00:09:00.120
with SDK installs, we
could potentially evolve

00:09:00.120 --> 00:09:04.610
that file format or its purpose
into something more ergonomic.

00:09:04.610 --> 00:09:07.850
Along the way, we want this
tool to cover features

00:09:07.850 --> 00:09:12.080
and functionality gaps that
system-level install users have

00:09:12.080 --> 00:09:16.320
that user-level installs do not.

00:09:16.320 --> 00:09:19.650
So earlier, when we were talking
about some of the pros and cons

00:09:19.650 --> 00:09:23.790
of the installation methods, I
mentioned that package managers

00:09:23.790 --> 00:09:27.750
and installers, like the
ones that Visual Studio use,

00:09:27.750 --> 00:09:32.280
have central management and
auditability kind of baked in

00:09:32.280 --> 00:09:36.440
and the current mechanisms for
user-level installs don't.

00:09:36.440 --> 00:09:39.890
So we want our tool to bring
that level of audibility

00:09:39.890 --> 00:09:42.360
and safety to everyone.

00:09:42.360 --> 00:09:45.000
And so to enable this,
we've been working

00:09:45.000 --> 00:09:50.370
with the.NET releases teams to
sign all of the binaries and all

00:09:50.370 --> 00:09:55.590
of the manifests involved so
that you know that the binaries

00:09:55.590 --> 00:09:58.260
that you are using are the ones
that we intended to deliver

00:09:58.260 --> 00:10:01.380
to you and all the way down.

00:10:01.380 --> 00:10:04.740
And then finally, we didn't
want this tool to exist

00:10:04.740 --> 00:10:07.320
in isolation, to be in a bubble.

00:10:07.320 --> 00:10:11.040
We wanted to create this tool
and create a library around it

00:10:11.040 --> 00:10:15.690
so that other tools that
needed to behave similarly

00:10:15.690 --> 00:10:20.940
or that needed to interact
with installs had the option

00:10:20.940 --> 00:10:24.840
of using a library that would
do things correctly and safely.

00:10:24.840 --> 00:10:29.850
So this will play a little bit
more into the long-term plans.

00:10:29.850 --> 00:10:33.000
But we hope that if this tool
takes off, the manifests

00:10:33.000 --> 00:10:36.420
and bookkeeping that it uses
become a shared resource

00:10:36.420 --> 00:10:41.070
for a constellation of tools
like Dependabot and Renovate

00:10:41.070 --> 00:10:43.650
and this whole audibility

00:10:43.650 --> 00:10:45.930
and automated compliance
infrastructure

00:10:45.930 --> 00:10:49.530
that many developers use today
for their package references

00:10:49.530 --> 00:10:51.870
and their Docker
file references.

00:10:51.870 --> 00:10:54.210
So we want to fit in that world.

00:10:54.210 --> 00:10:57.660
And part of fitting in that
world is providing tools to plug

00:10:57.660 --> 00:11:01.600
in easily into those systems.

00:11:02.640 --> 00:11:04.950
So from this point on, we're
going to talk about some

00:11:04.950 --> 00:11:07.830
of the specifics of dotnetup
and show it to you.

00:11:07.830 --> 00:11:09.840
But I want to be super clear.

00:11:09.840 --> 00:11:12.180
Everything you're
seeing from here

00:11:12.180 --> 00:11:15.870
on is internal preview level.

00:11:15.870 --> 00:11:17.520
We'll talk about some
of the milestones

00:11:17.520 --> 00:11:20.340
in this release process
a little bit later on.

00:11:20.340 --> 00:11:22.350
But I wanted to show
you what we have today

00:11:22.350 --> 00:11:26.220
so you can see the vision of how
it all works together and so

00:11:26.220 --> 00:11:31.020
that you can see where we're
aiming for and give us feedback,

00:11:31.020 --> 00:11:32.820
because the earlier
we get your feedback,

00:11:32.820 --> 00:11:34.170
the earlier we can
incorporate it,

00:11:34.170 --> 00:11:36.360
and the better the
end product will be.

00:11:36.360 --> 00:11:39.300
So big giant "Under
Construction" label here.

00:11:39.300 --> 00:11:42.150
If this were like the early
2000s, on a web page,

00:11:42.150 --> 00:11:44.700
you'd see the big
blinking marquee

00:11:44.700 --> 00:11:46.410
"Under Construction" sign.

00:11:46.410 --> 00:11:49.590
So just be aware of that.

00:11:49.590 --> 00:11:51.030
We're going to lean
into some demos now.

00:11:51.030 --> 00:11:52.980
And I'm sure the first
question on your mind is,

00:11:52.980 --> 00:11:54.660
how do I get this thing?

00:11:54.660 --> 00:11:57.840
So we're going to talk about the
acquisition and initialization

00:11:57.840 --> 00:12:00.690
of dotnetup on
different systems.

00:12:00.690 --> 00:12:03.600
Then we're going to show
how you can use dotnetup

00:12:03.600 --> 00:12:05.580
to easily get
started in a couple

00:12:05.580 --> 00:12:08.730
of different kinds
of repo shapes.

00:12:08.730 --> 00:12:12.090
And then finally, we'll talk
about how you can use dotnetup

00:12:12.090 --> 00:12:14.790
to tackle one of the
more annoying problems

00:12:14.790 --> 00:12:17.320
of toolchain management today.

00:12:18.480 --> 00:12:20.010
So let's get into it.

00:12:20.010 --> 00:12:24.990
I'm going to "Alt-Tab"
over to my terminal here.

00:12:24.990 --> 00:12:28.350
And where we are is in a repo
that I'll give you the link

00:12:28.350 --> 00:12:30.210
to a little bit later.

00:12:30.210 --> 00:12:33.540
I've got a repo where I've
put together some scenarios,

00:12:33.540 --> 00:12:35.880
some example repository patterns

00:12:35.880 --> 00:12:38.910
that might benefit
from dotnetup.

00:12:38.910 --> 00:12:41.820
So if we look in my
scenarios folder,

00:12:41.820 --> 00:12:45.060
I've got three scenarios here:
a "cross-platform-webapp,"

00:12:45.060 --> 00:12:48.360
a "multi-runtime-testing"
application,

00:12:48.360 --> 00:12:50.850
and then an application
that has up-to-date

00:12:50.850 --> 00:12:54.300
or outdated.NET
SDK installations.

00:12:54.300 --> 00:12:57.030
I want to start with the most
straightforward one, but first,

00:12:57.030 --> 00:12:59.040
I need to get dotnetup.

00:12:59.040 --> 00:13:02.280
And that's where we've tried
to make this really easy.

00:13:02.280 --> 00:13:05.850
We have aka.ms links that I will
again show a little bit later

00:13:05.850 --> 00:13:07.950
to make it easy to get dotnetup.

00:13:07.950 --> 00:13:12.090
You can go to
"aka.ms/dotnetup/get-dotnetup,"

00:13:12.090 --> 00:13:13.770
and there will be a Shell

00:13:13.770 --> 00:13:17.370
or PowerShell script
that you can use.

00:13:17.370 --> 00:13:20.460
And we'll have examples
for both of these

00:13:20.460 --> 00:13:23.160
in the documentation links
that you'll see later.

00:13:23.160 --> 00:13:25.980
But it's as easy as
running this script.

00:13:25.980 --> 00:13:28.830
And this script is going to
look at the platform you're on

00:13:28.830 --> 00:13:32.370
and determine which version of
dotnetup it should download.

00:13:32.370 --> 00:13:36.060
Because remember, this is an AOT
platform-specific application.

00:13:36.060 --> 00:13:38.820
We want this to be
as fast as possible.

00:13:38.820 --> 00:13:42.450
We will download it, verify
it, and then install it

00:13:42.450 --> 00:13:45.090
into your home
directory by default.

00:13:45.090 --> 00:13:48.150
Though, as always,
you have 100% control

00:13:48.150 --> 00:13:50.580
over where this thing goes.

00:13:50.580 --> 00:13:55.110
So path isn't configured because
that's a little hard to do

00:13:55.110 --> 00:13:58.110
in a script that you
source like this on Linux.

00:13:58.110 --> 00:14:03.090
So we'll just start by
invoking it manually.

00:14:03.090 --> 00:14:07.500
And the first time
you run dotnetup

00:14:07.500 --> 00:14:11.460
or when you run the init command
specifically, you'll get run

00:14:11.460 --> 00:14:13.680
through a configuration
walkthrough

00:14:13.680 --> 00:14:16.800
where dotnetup itself will
help you set your system

00:14:16.800 --> 00:14:18.870
up for success.

00:14:18.870 --> 00:14:21.420
So I just want to call
out here that dotnetup,

00:14:21.420 --> 00:14:24.270
like the.NET CLI, collects
the usage telemetry

00:14:24.270 --> 00:14:26.610
to help us understand
how you use it.

00:14:26.610 --> 00:14:29.130
But just like the.NET
CLI, you can use all

00:14:29.130 --> 00:14:33.030
of the existing telemetry
opt-out flags that we have

00:14:33.030 --> 00:14:35.640
in the.NET CLI to
control that experience.

00:14:35.640 --> 00:14:37.920
So just a heads up there.

00:14:37.920 --> 00:14:39.780
The first time you run
dotnetup, like I said,

00:14:39.780 --> 00:14:41.430
you'll get this walkthrough.

00:14:41.430 --> 00:14:44.400
And it wants to ensure
that you are set

00:14:44.400 --> 00:14:47.580
up with a viable
installation.NET

00:14:47.580 --> 00:14:50.400
that you can use to
start development.

00:14:50.400 --> 00:14:53.100
It's going to prompt you
for the different channels

00:14:53.100 --> 00:14:54.420
that you might want to use.

00:14:54.420 --> 00:14:57.030
And because I'm a
bleeding-edge kind of person,

00:14:57.030 --> 00:14:58.620
I'm going to choose "latest"

00:14:58.620 --> 00:15:00.690
so that we will get the
latest stable release

00:15:00.690 --> 00:15:02.040
that exists today.

00:15:02.040 --> 00:15:05.610
And then we will stay
up to date over time.

00:15:05.610 --> 00:15:09.780
The next question is how you
would like to use dotnetup.

00:15:09.780 --> 00:15:13.950
This could be a bit
of a deep dive,

00:15:13.950 --> 00:15:17.190
but the way that
matches most closely

00:15:17.190 --> 00:15:20.490
to how you might be expecting
this to work from tools

00:15:20.490 --> 00:15:24.060
like DNVM or Mise-en-Place
is terminal mode.

00:15:24.060 --> 00:15:25.050
So I'm going to use this.

00:15:25.050 --> 00:15:28.980
And in this mode, dotnetup
is going to configure itself

00:15:28.980 --> 00:15:31.380
to add itself to the path

00:15:31.380 --> 00:15:33.870
and to add the.NET
installation location

00:15:33.870 --> 00:15:36.540
that it manages to the path.

00:15:36.540 --> 00:15:40.980
And I'm going to skip that last
question because dotnetup saw

00:15:40.980 --> 00:15:44.280
that I had global installs
and offered to port them

00:15:44.280 --> 00:15:46.440
over to the new world for me.

00:15:46.440 --> 00:15:51.210
But when I told it what to
install, it went to grab it,

00:15:51.210 --> 00:15:54.600
it went to install it
into its managed.NET

00:15:54.600 --> 00:15:56.130
installation location.

00:15:56.130 --> 00:15:58.320
And then it gave me
some nice UI and UX

00:15:58.320 --> 00:16:01.980
around the downloading
installation of that SDK.

00:16:01.980 --> 00:16:06.330
So at this point, it has
downloaded the latest.NET SDK

00:16:06.330 --> 00:16:11.610
for me, and it has updated
my shell profile so that

00:16:11.610 --> 00:16:18.510
on subsequent indications,
my shell will include all

00:16:18.510 --> 00:16:23.940
of the environment variables
and configuration necessary

00:16:23.940 --> 00:16:26.910
to successfully run dotnetup.

00:16:26.910 --> 00:16:29.730
So if we go look at the
command that it inserted

00:16:29.730 --> 00:16:33.180
into my profile, this is
a shell-specific script.

00:16:33.180 --> 00:16:37.650
It detected I'm using Z
shell, that sets up.NET

00:16:37.650 --> 00:16:41.130
to use the installation
that dotnetup manages

00:16:41.130 --> 00:16:44.730
and then ensures that
dotnetup and the.NET XE are

00:16:44.730 --> 00:16:46.920
on my path, which is great.

00:16:46.920 --> 00:16:51.630
That means I can now
do "dotnetup -- info."

00:16:51.630 --> 00:16:55.980
Well, if I were in a new shell
session, I could do that.

00:16:55.980 --> 00:17:00.210
So I can do "dotnetup -- info"
here, and I can see that I'm

00:17:00.210 --> 00:17:02.730
on a specific
version of dotnetup.

00:17:02.730 --> 00:17:07.440
And then I can
do.NET info as well.

00:17:07.440 --> 00:17:10.050
And because this is the
first time that version

00:17:10.050 --> 00:17:12.150
of that is run, it will
do some crunching.

00:17:12.150 --> 00:17:13.770
And then it will
tell me everything

00:17:13.770 --> 00:17:21.660
about this installation, which,
as we asked for, is 10.0.300,

00:17:21.660 --> 00:17:27.450
is on macOS, is installed to a
location that dotnetup manages,

00:17:27.450 --> 00:17:31.650
and comes with the ASP.NET
and NETCORE runtimes.

00:17:31.650 --> 00:17:35.820
So this is exactly what anyone
coming to.NET would need.

00:17:35.820 --> 00:17:41.310
And it was as easy as "dotnetup
init," and we're good.

00:17:41.310 --> 00:17:45.150
So now that I have dotnetup,
I'm prepared to work

00:17:45.150 --> 00:17:47.940
on any repo that I want.

00:17:47.940 --> 00:17:52.110
So if we go into the
"cross-platform-webapp"

00:17:52.110 --> 00:17:58.170
that I wanted to show you all,
this web app is very simple.

00:17:58.170 --> 00:18:02.880
It has a library and a web app
that consumes that library.

00:18:02.880 --> 00:18:08.790
But it also has a "global.json"
file that I want to show you.

00:18:08.790 --> 00:18:11.460
And this global JSON
has something different

00:18:11.460 --> 00:18:13.320
to what I just installed.

00:18:13.320 --> 00:18:16.710
This global JSON says
that this repo expects

00:18:16.710 --> 00:18:20.220
to use 10.0.100 of the.NET SDK.

00:18:20.220 --> 00:18:21.510
Why? I don't know.

00:18:21.510 --> 00:18:23.970
But this is what the
developer has chosen.

00:18:23.970 --> 00:18:25.890
But it's okay with
rolling forward

00:18:25.890 --> 00:18:27.750
to the latestFeature band.

00:18:27.750 --> 00:18:30.000
So we're going to we're
going to use that.

00:18:30.000 --> 00:18:32.790
And it turns out dotnet
up understands that.

00:18:32.790 --> 00:18:45.750
If we run "dotnetup sdk
install" here -- Yeah.

00:18:45.750 --> 00:18:48.150
Oh, it rolled forward to
the latestFeature band

00:18:48.150 --> 00:18:50.730
because that's what's
in the global JSON.

00:18:50.730 --> 00:18:55.500
But if we open up our
"global.json" in VS Code here

00:18:55.500 --> 00:18:59.610
and we change this to
be something like,

00:18:59.610 --> 00:19:03.240
we'll do "latestPatch"
instead, and then we come back

00:19:03.240 --> 00:19:07.350
to run our install, then we can
see that dotnetup has chosen

00:19:07.350 --> 00:19:13.170
to install 1xx, so meaning
the 100 feature band.

00:19:13.170 --> 00:19:16.800
And it even tells us why,
because the global JSON

00:19:16.800 --> 00:19:20.040
in our project
specifies that version.

00:19:20.040 --> 00:19:22.740
And so here we can also see
the download and the install

00:19:22.740 --> 00:19:27.390
of this new version of
the.NET SDK, and we're good.

00:19:27.390 --> 00:19:34.620
So now that we are in our repo,
we can go run our web app

00:19:34.620 --> 00:19:38.670
or build it, and
everything will just work.

00:19:38.670 --> 00:19:42.000
It'll use the SDK
that we told it to,

00:19:42.000 --> 00:19:43.980
and it will build the app.

00:19:43.980 --> 00:19:47.130
And I didn't have to
do anything crazy.

00:19:47.130 --> 00:19:50.060
All I had to do was
run "dotnetup install"

00:19:50.060 --> 00:19:52.830
or "dotnetup sdk install."

00:19:52.830 --> 00:19:56.370
And with that one command,
I was good to go.

00:19:56.370 --> 00:20:01.530
This is the core value
proposition that we see.

00:20:01.530 --> 00:20:06.180
And so here's me proving
that we used the 108 SDK.

00:20:06.180 --> 00:20:08.730
This is the core value
proposition of dotnetup.

00:20:08.730 --> 00:20:11.090
You could do that same gesture:

00:20:11.090 --> 00:20:13.220
grabbing the dotnetup
installation,

00:20:13.220 --> 00:20:17.090
running "dotnetup init" to
get a dotnetup install going,

00:20:17.090 --> 00:20:23.420
and then "dotnetup install" on
any repo and have a.NET SDK

00:20:23.420 --> 00:20:25.710
that will work for that repo.

00:20:27.710 --> 00:20:29.000
That's great.

00:20:29.000 --> 00:20:32.550
It is very frustrating to have
to manage those things today.

00:20:32.550 --> 00:20:36.240
And while there are some helpers
that will use a global JSON

00:20:36.240 --> 00:20:39.960
in CI/CD, for example,
like in GitHub actions,

00:20:39.960 --> 00:20:44.910
the actions/setup-dotnet
action will do that for you.

00:20:44.910 --> 00:20:49.470
There's been nothing supported
locally that you could use

00:20:49.470 --> 00:20:54.000
on your developer box or that an
agent could use on your behalf.

00:20:54.000 --> 00:20:57.720
So we're trying to fill that
need here in a supported way.

00:20:57.720 --> 00:21:01.470
Dotnetup does more than just
SDK installations, though.

00:21:01.470 --> 00:21:04.890
If you list this, if you run
the dotnetup list command,

00:21:04.890 --> 00:21:08.940
you can see that it's actually
tracking those decisions

00:21:08.940 --> 00:21:11.490
that you made and why
you've made them.

00:21:11.490 --> 00:21:13.770
So it knows that it
has two versions

00:21:13.770 --> 00:21:16.200
of the.NET SDK installed.

00:21:16.200 --> 00:21:20.430
And it knows what channels
you are tracking.

00:21:20.430 --> 00:21:23.040
So it's tracking
the latest channel

00:21:23.040 --> 00:21:27.510
because I explicitly told
it to on that startup.

00:21:27.510 --> 00:21:32.370
It's tracking the 1xx
channel because our webapp,

00:21:32.370 --> 00:21:34.440
"global.json," told it to.

00:21:34.440 --> 00:21:41.790
And that's good because it means
that when a new 109 is released

00:21:41.790 --> 00:21:46.410
in May or a 110 in June, and
so on, I guess 108 was May,

00:21:46.410 --> 00:21:51.180
so a 109 in June and a 110
in July, and so forth,

00:21:51.180 --> 00:21:52.590
dotnetup will understand that.

00:21:52.590 --> 00:21:59.850
And a dotnetup update will
do those updates for you.

00:21:59.850 --> 00:22:01.590
So this is the real beauty.

00:22:01.590 --> 00:22:04.620
It's not just installations.

00:22:04.620 --> 00:22:08.970
It's understanding what
you need and what you have

00:22:08.970 --> 00:22:12.420
and what is available and
making those things all

00:22:12.420 --> 00:22:14.730
align consistently.

00:22:14.730 --> 00:22:20.220
So that's the first end-to-end
example I wanted to show you.

00:22:20.220 --> 00:22:24.510
And this is something that, if
you are engaged in open source

00:22:24.510 --> 00:22:27.240
or if you are using
agents to help contribute

00:22:27.240 --> 00:22:29.640
to open source repos
that you use at work

00:22:29.640 --> 00:22:31.830
or whatever your pattern is,

00:22:31.830 --> 00:22:33.750
that's a pattern you
probably had to do before.

00:22:33.750 --> 00:22:36.900
You had to go clone the
repo and then figure out,

00:22:36.900 --> 00:22:39.840
like you just try a normal
dot net build, and the builds

00:22:39.840 --> 00:22:43.560
or the SDKs you have don't
match with what's in the repo.

00:22:43.560 --> 00:22:48.750
So that problem, we can say,
is solved definitively.

00:22:48.750 --> 00:22:52.440
The next scenario is a little
more interesting and, I think,

00:22:52.440 --> 00:22:57.840
shows off more of the potential
future direction for dotnetup.

00:22:57.840 --> 00:22:59.190
And for this one,
I'm going to need

00:22:59.190 --> 00:23:02.520
to show you a little bit
of code to set it up.

00:23:02.520 --> 00:23:05.580
So we have a repo here,

00:23:05.580 --> 00:23:12.870
a workspace where we have
a source code library

00:23:12.870 --> 00:23:15.600
that provides some utility
that we care about.

00:23:15.600 --> 00:23:19.860
And then we have a test
project that consumes it.

00:23:19.860 --> 00:23:23.370
So we have a really cool
truncation string helper here

00:23:23.370 --> 00:23:25.470
and a string helper
that can check

00:23:25.470 --> 00:23:30.060
if a given string is only
alphanumeric ASCII characters.

00:23:30.060 --> 00:23:31.740
So kind of basic stuff.

00:23:31.740 --> 00:23:34.650
And like responsible developers,

00:23:34.650 --> 00:23:37.890
we've created a set
of test cases for it.

00:23:37.890 --> 00:23:41.790
And so we want to be
able to test this.

00:23:41.790 --> 00:23:46.110
Well, what's interesting is
that, like many library authors,

00:23:46.110 --> 00:23:51.120
we might want to do some
customization of that library

00:23:51.120 --> 00:23:53.220
for different target frameworks.

00:23:53.220 --> 00:23:54.990
For example, in.NET 10,

00:23:54.990 --> 00:24:00.600
maybe there is a new span-based
overload of index of string

00:24:00.600 --> 00:24:04.740
that we want to make use
of that we can't on.NET 8.

00:24:04.740 --> 00:24:08.850
And so in our library, we might
have an #ifdef that we add

00:24:08.850 --> 00:24:14.400
in here that we use a different
execution path on 10 versus 8.

00:24:14.400 --> 00:24:17.850
But we still want to build for 8
because we want people that are

00:24:17.850 --> 00:24:21.000
on 8 to be able to
use our library.

00:24:21.000 --> 00:24:22.950
We call this multi-targeting.

00:24:22.950 --> 00:24:25.530
And from a library perspective,

00:24:25.530 --> 00:24:28.410
it doesn't actually matter
what you target here.

00:24:28.410 --> 00:24:31.740
So here in my library,
I'm using NetStandard 2.0

00:24:31.740 --> 00:24:36.810
because my library doesn't do
any per runtime specialization.

00:24:36.810 --> 00:24:40.200
But as a library author, I want
to make sure that everyone

00:24:40.200 --> 00:24:42.690
on the latest and
greatest versions

00:24:42.690 --> 00:24:45.570
of.NET can use my library.

00:24:45.570 --> 00:24:51.390
And so a way we could do that
is by making sample projects

00:24:51.390 --> 00:24:54.060
that consume my
library and use it.

00:24:54.060 --> 00:24:56.430
But a more integrated
way is to write tests.

00:24:56.430 --> 00:24:57.660
And so that's what
we've done here.

00:24:57.660 --> 00:24:59.220
We have a test library

00:24:59.220 --> 00:25:02.700
that targets these three
different in-support runtimes

00:25:02.700 --> 00:25:04.750
and just has these tests.

00:25:06.120 --> 00:25:11.550
Something I've struggled with
often in my career is I want

00:25:11.550 --> 00:25:18.210
to use the latest.NET SDK.

00:25:18.210 --> 00:25:23.040
Because the SDK is where all
the tooling features come from.

00:25:23.040 --> 00:25:27.030
So, regardless of what runtime
I'm building assets for,

00:25:27.030 --> 00:25:30.630
I want to use a 10 SDK right now

00:25:30.630 --> 00:25:33.600
because that's the latest
major version out there.

00:25:33.600 --> 00:25:37.050
So I end up in a situation
where I have one SDK,

00:25:37.050 --> 00:25:41.400
but I need multiple
runtimes to run my tests.

00:25:41.400 --> 00:25:48.060
And if I try this right now and
I come over to my test and I try

00:25:48.060 --> 00:25:50.310
and run -- Well, before
we get into this,

00:25:50.310 --> 00:25:52.560
I'm going to go "--
list-runtimes."

00:25:52.560 --> 00:25:56.130
And you can see here that
we just have 10 runtimes.

00:25:56.130 --> 00:25:59.070
And I'm going to run
"dotnet test" on my test,

00:25:59.070 --> 00:26:02.460
which targets 8, 9, and 10.

00:26:02.460 --> 00:26:04.680
And that took no
time at all to run,

00:26:04.680 --> 00:26:06.180
but it didn't run successfully.

00:26:06.180 --> 00:26:08.460
I'm going to zoom out a
little bit so you can see it.

00:26:08.460 --> 00:26:12.360
But what happened was
on.NET 8, no tests ran

00:26:12.360 --> 00:26:18.060
because we did not have a.NET
Core Runtime for version 8.

00:26:18.060 --> 00:26:21.390
And then for.NET 9, no tests ran

00:26:21.390 --> 00:26:26.070
because we did not have
a.NET Core Runtime for 9.

00:26:26.070 --> 00:26:30.840
And then on.NET 10, our tests
did run, and they passed.

00:26:30.840 --> 00:26:33.060
This is something I
see all the time.

00:26:33.060 --> 00:26:36.750
And when people see this,
the way they fix it is

00:26:36.750 --> 00:26:38.700
in their CI/CD providers,

00:26:38.700 --> 00:26:44.340
they install entirely
parallel SDK installations.

00:26:44.340 --> 00:26:50.310
So if you ran this command
on their CI setups,

00:26:50.310 --> 00:26:55.710
you would see an 8 SDK, a 9
SDK, and one or more 10 SDKs.

00:26:55.710 --> 00:26:58.530
And to me, that's not necessary

00:26:58.530 --> 00:27:00.870
because new SDKs
include new tooling

00:27:00.870 --> 00:27:04.440
and fully support
building older runtimes.

00:27:04.440 --> 00:27:08.400
So instead, what I would
like to do is just be able

00:27:08.400 --> 00:27:12.930
to get runtimes that I need to
actually run my application.

00:27:12.930 --> 00:27:16.350
And this is not something
that you can do easily today.

00:27:16.350 --> 00:27:19.950
So one of the things
that dotnetup kind

00:27:19.950 --> 00:27:22.350
of makes easy is
installing runtimes.

00:27:22.350 --> 00:27:25.290
And so I've done a
little shenanigans here,

00:27:25.290 --> 00:27:28.530
and I've made a fake
extension to Global JSON,

00:27:28.530 --> 00:27:30.930
where I can say I
need some runtimes,

00:27:30.930 --> 00:27:34.350
and I want the.NET runtime,
and I need 8, 9, and 10.

00:27:34.350 --> 00:27:36.930
This syntax is not
final by any means.

00:27:36.930 --> 00:27:40.560
This is just something that
might happen in the future.

00:27:40.560 --> 00:27:44.100
And there's a world where
dotnetup would understand that.

00:27:44.100 --> 00:27:48.450
And it would, when you
ran dotnetup install,

00:27:48.450 --> 00:27:51.180
it would do that for you.

00:27:51.180 --> 00:27:54.510
It would install both the SDKs
and the runtimes that you need.

00:27:54.510 --> 00:27:56.610
We're not in that world today.

00:27:56.610 --> 00:27:58.710
And so instead, what you
can do is you can do

00:27:58.710 --> 00:28:03.990
"dotnet runtime
install 8.0 9.0 10.0,"

00:28:03.990 --> 00:28:05.940
and it will do them
all at the same time.

00:28:05.940 --> 00:28:09.120
It'll go find the latest
ones of each of those bands,

00:28:09.120 --> 00:28:11.340
download them, and install them.

00:28:11.340 --> 00:28:15.840
In this case, I just
said 8.0, 9.0, and 10.0,

00:28:15.840 --> 00:28:19.650
and dotnetup got me the latest
one of each of those bands.

00:28:19.650 --> 00:28:23.730
If I had specific needs, I
could say specific versions.

00:28:23.730 --> 00:28:25.350
That's not a problem.

00:28:25.350 --> 00:28:29.460
But it's very easy now to
acquire these runtimes.

00:28:29.460 --> 00:28:32.040
And now that I have
these runtimes,

00:28:32.040 --> 00:28:37.050
I can go run my tests
again, and they'll work.

00:28:37.050 --> 00:28:41.880
And I didn't have to install
entire new SDKs at hundreds

00:28:41.880 --> 00:28:45.420
of megabytes apiece just
to make my tests run.

00:28:45.420 --> 00:28:47.460
This, to me, is the
best of both worlds.

00:28:47.460 --> 00:28:50.910
You're on the latest tooling,
but you're able to do the things

00:28:50.910 --> 00:28:55.350
that you need to do in ways that
you couldn't before easily.

00:28:55.350 --> 00:28:57.390
So this is exciting to me.

00:28:57.390 --> 00:28:59.280
It's bringing some of the
convenience of things

00:28:59.280 --> 00:29:01.890
that you could do in
your CI/CD setups

00:29:01.890 --> 00:29:03.870
to your local development.

00:29:03.870 --> 00:29:07.890
So that's another example of
the kinds of functionality

00:29:07.890 --> 00:29:09.660
that dotnetup can provide.

00:29:09.660 --> 00:29:13.710
And even though I'm just
showing the.NET runtime here,

00:29:13.710 --> 00:29:18.580
you can specify all kinds of
different specifiers here.

00:29:19.710 --> 00:29:21.970
You can do ASP.NET.

00:29:24.000 --> 00:29:26.250
So here's an example
of aspnetcore.

00:29:26.250 --> 00:29:31.560
You can also do Windows desktop
on Windows in that case.

00:29:31.560 --> 00:29:32.580
So that would look like

00:29:32.580 --> 00:29:37.270
"dotnetup runtime
install aspnetcore@8.0."

00:29:38.670 --> 00:29:42.030
And this pattern of component
name @version is one

00:29:42.030 --> 00:29:46.200
that we've been fleshing out
over a few SDK releases here.

00:29:46.200 --> 00:29:48.540
And now we have that runtime.

00:29:48.540 --> 00:29:51.570
So if we run "dotnet
-- list-runtimes" now,

00:29:51.570 --> 00:29:55.500
you'll see that we have the
core runtimes for 8, 9, and 10,

00:29:55.500 --> 00:29:58.110
and the ASP.NET
runtimes for 8 and 10.

00:29:58.110 --> 00:30:01.290
Easy peasy.

00:30:01.290 --> 00:30:02.040
That's it.

00:30:02.040 --> 00:30:05.970
So that's the core value prop.

00:30:05.970 --> 00:30:09.150
Can I get tooling more easily?

00:30:09.150 --> 00:30:11.010
But it's also
interesting to note

00:30:11.010 --> 00:30:13.620
that getting the tooling
is only half the battle.

00:30:13.620 --> 00:30:16.380
There is a long tail of
maintenance and upgrades

00:30:16.380 --> 00:30:20.520
that responsible development
teams have to adhere to.

00:30:20.520 --> 00:30:23.610
And dotnetup makes
that possible as well.

00:30:23.610 --> 00:30:27.030
So we saw dotnetup list before,
but I just want to call

00:30:27.030 --> 00:30:28.320
that up here again to note

00:30:28.320 --> 00:30:32.970
that those explicit runtimes
are also tracked here.

00:30:32.970 --> 00:30:35.250
And you can see what is
currently installed,

00:30:35.250 --> 00:30:38.970
and you can see the
requests here as well.

00:30:38.970 --> 00:30:42.180
So when new versions of
the runtimes release,

00:30:42.180 --> 00:30:48.600
a simple "dotnetup update"
will cover them as well.

00:30:48.600 --> 00:30:51.870
And because I just
installed all of these,

00:30:51.870 --> 00:30:53.130
they're all already up to date.

00:30:53.130 --> 00:30:58.170
But if any one of these were out
of date compared to the requests

00:30:58.170 --> 00:31:02.220
that we had, dotnetup would have
installed them right there.

00:31:02.220 --> 00:31:03.840
This is all happening
under the covers

00:31:03.840 --> 00:31:06.690
because dotnetup is
keeping a manifest,

00:31:06.690 --> 00:31:11.430
an installation tracker
in your user directory

00:31:11.430 --> 00:31:12.990
in a data directory there.

00:31:12.990 --> 00:31:16.680
So you can think of it as a
lock file for the declared

00:31:16.680 --> 00:31:18.930
or desired state of your system.

00:31:18.930 --> 00:31:21.810
It's not necessarily something
that we intend to share

00:31:21.810 --> 00:31:24.180
across repos or
across developers.

00:31:24.180 --> 00:31:25.650
You should still use global JSON

00:31:25.650 --> 00:31:29.430
to describe your requirements
for development.

00:31:29.430 --> 00:31:31.530
But that manifests as
what's powering a lot

00:31:31.530 --> 00:31:34.800
of this update
information as well.

00:31:34.800 --> 00:31:36.990
So those are some of the
core functionalities there.

00:31:36.990 --> 00:31:39.030
There are a few other
interesting things

00:31:39.030 --> 00:31:43.050
about dotnetup that I
want to call out here.

00:31:43.050 --> 00:31:47.520
The "print-env-script" is a
helper command that can be used

00:31:47.520 --> 00:31:51.780
to make sure your shell
is configured correctly

00:31:51.780 --> 00:31:53.850
to just natively use dotnetup.

00:31:53.850 --> 00:31:57.180
This is what is used
when you do that init

00:31:57.180 --> 00:32:00.270
to configure your
system to use dotnetup.

00:32:00.270 --> 00:32:06.420
Right now, this print-env-script
is supported for Bash and Zsh

00:32:06.420 --> 00:32:09.810
and other POSIX shells only.

00:32:09.810 --> 00:32:11.220
PowerShell support is coming.

00:32:11.220 --> 00:32:13.290
We have that in PR.

00:32:13.290 --> 00:32:15.360
And hopefully, within a
week or two from the time

00:32:15.360 --> 00:32:16.920
that you're seeing
this, you'll be able

00:32:16.920 --> 00:32:20.470
to grab dotnetup yourself
and give that a try.

00:32:21.630 --> 00:32:23.550
The other thing
that's interesting

00:32:23.550 --> 00:32:28.500
that has been missing from SDK
management is uninstallations.

00:32:28.500 --> 00:32:33.360
So when you do these updates,
new versions of SDKs

00:32:33.360 --> 00:32:36.240
and runtimes will be
installed on your system.

00:32:36.240 --> 00:32:40.410
But that means the old ones
may not actually be needed

00:32:40.410 --> 00:32:42.330
by any tools anymore.

00:32:42.330 --> 00:32:46.350
So an update will remove
out-of-date installations

00:32:46.350 --> 00:32:49.740
that are no longer needed
by the set of requirements

00:32:49.740 --> 00:32:51.300
that you have declared.

00:32:51.300 --> 00:32:54.600
You can also manually
uninstall SDKs

00:32:54.600 --> 00:32:57.180
and runtimes if you need them.

00:32:57.180 --> 00:32:59.340
Architecturally, I
want to call out here

00:32:59.340 --> 00:33:02.430
that dotnetup has a
top-level install update

00:33:02.430 --> 00:33:03.720
and uninstall command.

00:33:03.720 --> 00:33:07.290
But it also has
component-level commands,

00:33:07.290 --> 00:33:09.600
"dotnetup sdk install,"

00:33:09.600 --> 00:33:13.050
"dotnetup sdk update,"
and so forth.

00:33:13.050 --> 00:33:16.560
This is because we are
seeing dotnetup as a way

00:33:16.560 --> 00:33:21.780
to start thinking about
the entire.NET toolchain

00:33:21.780 --> 00:33:23.700
in terms of components.

00:33:23.700 --> 00:33:26.070
Right now, the two
clean lines we have

00:33:26.070 --> 00:33:28.950
for components are
SDKs and runtimes.

00:33:28.950 --> 00:33:32.880
But there are features and
functionality of the SDK

00:33:32.880 --> 00:33:34.260
that might make more sense

00:33:34.260 --> 00:33:37.230
as discrete components
for you to use.

00:33:37.230 --> 00:33:39.000
Those of you who are
familiar with tools

00:33:39.000 --> 00:33:42.270
like rustup may see
where this is going.

00:33:42.270 --> 00:33:46.200
You can imagine a world where
pieces of the SDK might be able

00:33:46.200 --> 00:33:49.680
to release at
different cadences.

00:33:49.680 --> 00:33:51.780
And instead of
drowning in a bunch

00:33:51.780 --> 00:33:55.290
of different update
notifications and inability

00:33:55.290 --> 00:33:58.500
to control that rate of
change, you, as a developer,

00:33:58.500 --> 00:34:02.220
could use dotnetup to stay on
top of that and have one command

00:34:02.220 --> 00:34:06.750
to manage all of your
updates and components.

00:34:06.750 --> 00:34:12.690
So that's where I think we'll
stop with the demos themselves.

00:34:12.690 --> 00:34:16.020
And we'll switch back over
to go talk a little bit more

00:34:16.020 --> 00:34:20.730
about what the future of
dotnetup will look like.

00:34:20.730 --> 00:34:23.190
And to talk about future
development, first,

00:34:23.190 --> 00:34:26.250
I want to talk about who
we're designing this for.

00:34:26.250 --> 00:34:29.600
There are two primary
consumers here.

00:34:29.600 --> 00:34:33.740
The first is humans, and the
second is agents of all kinds.

00:34:33.740 --> 00:34:36.740
Not just the AI or
LLM style of agent,

00:34:36.740 --> 00:34:42.090
but CI/CD systems are
agents in a way as well.

00:34:42.090 --> 00:34:46.740
So really, it's humans
and automated use cases.

00:34:46.740 --> 00:34:49.560
We want humans to be
able to use this tool.

00:34:49.560 --> 00:34:52.650
And we want their expectations
from other ecosystems

00:34:52.650 --> 00:34:54.210
to carry over broadly.

00:34:54.210 --> 00:34:59.760
We want to fill a need
for simple management

00:34:59.760 --> 00:35:03.300
and consistent management of
the parts of our platform.

00:35:03.300 --> 00:35:06.090
We don't want someone to have to
learn a bunch of new concepts.

00:35:06.090 --> 00:35:08.580
We want them to just be
able to clone a repo,

00:35:08.580 --> 00:35:11.910
run dotnetup install,
and be good to go.

00:35:11.910 --> 00:35:15.990
And we want that user experience
to be something that someone

00:35:15.990 --> 00:35:18.810
on any OS platform can use.

00:35:18.810 --> 00:35:23.430
So this entire time I've been
talking about users using this.

00:35:23.430 --> 00:35:26.490
I'm meaning developers
specifically.

00:35:26.490 --> 00:35:28.020
This is a developer time tool

00:35:28.020 --> 00:35:31.050
to make developer time
use cases more simple

00:35:31.050 --> 00:35:33.600
and easy and straightforward.

00:35:33.600 --> 00:35:35.700
For your production use
cases, you would still want

00:35:35.700 --> 00:35:40.830
to use your package manager
to grab.NET runtimes

00:35:40.830 --> 00:35:43.050
for your framework-dependent
applications.

00:35:43.050 --> 00:35:44.700
Or you'd want to
bundle self-contained

00:35:44.700 --> 00:35:47.370
if that's the deployment
mechanism you prefer.

00:35:47.370 --> 00:35:51.720
Both of those are better options
for production deployments

00:35:51.720 --> 00:35:54.210
because with package
manager packages,

00:35:54.210 --> 00:35:56.910
you can get cross-system-wide
security updates

00:35:56.910 --> 00:35:58.920
as soon as they're available.

00:35:58.920 --> 00:36:00.690
Those of you doing
self-contained deployments

00:36:00.690 --> 00:36:03.570
already know that you have to
be on the ball every month

00:36:03.570 --> 00:36:06.390
for security releases to
roll out new versions.

00:36:06.390 --> 00:36:09.720
So production is not what we're
talking about with this tool.

00:36:09.720 --> 00:36:14.070
We're talking about developer
time use cases for humans.

00:36:14.070 --> 00:36:18.510
Agents similarly
bring expectations.

00:36:18.510 --> 00:36:20.610
In their case, their
expectations are a combination

00:36:20.610 --> 00:36:23.070
of what they were trained
on and what the skills

00:36:23.070 --> 00:36:25.770
and context they've
been given say.

00:36:25.770 --> 00:36:28.320
And so we want agents to
have something that looks

00:36:28.320 --> 00:36:31.620
and feels similar to other tools

00:36:31.620 --> 00:36:35.070
but adapts itself a little
bit more cleanly to some

00:36:35.070 --> 00:36:37.380
of the specific needs
of our ecosystems.

00:36:37.380 --> 00:36:39.210
So we want to give it a tool

00:36:39.210 --> 00:36:43.410
that the agent can use
successfully, quickly,

00:36:43.410 --> 00:36:46.890
and importantly, so
that it can do setup

00:36:46.890 --> 00:36:48.900
and management the right way.

00:36:48.900 --> 00:36:51.870
Because it's very possible for
an agent to go look at some docs

00:36:51.870 --> 00:36:56.430
and get half the story and unzip
a zip install on your system

00:36:56.430 --> 00:37:00.720
in a way that negatively impacts
your development experience.

00:37:00.720 --> 00:37:05.130
So we hope this tool can provide
guardrails for those agents

00:37:05.130 --> 00:37:10.410
in a way that ad hoc downloading
and execution wouldn't do.

00:37:10.410 --> 00:37:13.860
Agents also tend to work
best without admin rights,

00:37:13.860 --> 00:37:16.680
in sandboxes with guardrails.

00:37:16.680 --> 00:37:20.480
And so we needed a tool
that would work outside

00:37:20.480 --> 00:37:22.550
of a user elevation context,

00:37:22.550 --> 00:37:26.270
which then leads naturally
towards using your user

00:37:26.270 --> 00:37:28.740
directory in this way.

00:37:28.740 --> 00:37:32.040
Both of these categories of
agents need to stay up to date

00:37:32.040 --> 00:37:34.440
to get security fixes and
tooling enhancements.

00:37:34.440 --> 00:37:37.950
And they need to be able
to exercise better control

00:37:37.950 --> 00:37:39.330
over their environments.

00:37:39.330 --> 00:37:42.060
They need to be able
to say semantically,

00:37:42.060 --> 00:37:45.120
I want whatever the
latest stable version is,

00:37:45.120 --> 00:37:48.990
or I have a constraint
that says I need to stay

00:37:48.990 --> 00:37:53.430
on the latest 10
series of tools.

00:37:53.430 --> 00:37:59.400
So we want you to be able to
express those constraints

00:37:59.400 --> 00:38:02.310
and then have tooling respect
them locally the same way

00:38:02.310 --> 00:38:06.480
that CI/CD tooling
respects them today.

00:38:06.480 --> 00:38:10.230
So I've shown some things
that it can do today.

00:38:10.230 --> 00:38:12.330
I want to look a little bit
more towards the future

00:38:12.330 --> 00:38:14.610
as we get closer to
internal and public preview

00:38:14.610 --> 00:38:17.120
and beyond milestones.

00:38:17.120 --> 00:38:20.660
What I showed today was
management of stable SDKs

00:38:20.660 --> 00:38:24.320
and manual management
of runtimes.

00:38:24.320 --> 00:38:27.690
The runtime installations,

00:38:27.690 --> 00:38:30.180
you didn't have a global JSON
way of dealing with that.

00:38:30.180 --> 00:38:33.920
So you had to explicitly
say, I need this version.

00:38:33.920 --> 00:38:38.690
So we want to, in internal
preview, focus on managing SDKs.

00:38:38.690 --> 00:38:40.280
And we have a PR in the pipeline

00:38:40.280 --> 00:38:44.330
that will even let you
manage daily SDKs.

00:38:44.330 --> 00:38:48.900
So those of you who are
leading edge may know

00:38:48.900 --> 00:38:51.180
that we have these
monthly previews

00:38:51.180 --> 00:38:54.000
of the next major
version of.NET.

00:38:54.000 --> 00:38:59.550
So this month we released.NET
11, Preview 5, for example,

00:38:59.550 --> 00:39:01.080
or I guess 4, actually.

00:39:01.080 --> 00:39:03.460
We're working on 5 right now.

00:39:04.610 --> 00:39:07.490
Those previews, we
consider them stable

00:39:07.490 --> 00:39:11.490
from a release management
process perspective.

00:39:11.490 --> 00:39:16.440
Separately from those previews,
every build that goes

00:39:16.440 --> 00:39:21.150
into the.NET unified build
system produces a workable,

00:39:21.150 --> 00:39:23.490
usable SDK.

00:39:23.490 --> 00:39:26.160
And those can sometimes
provide bug fixes

00:39:26.160 --> 00:39:29.010
or sometimes provide new
features and functionality

00:39:29.010 --> 00:39:31.740
that we would like
fast feedback on.

00:39:31.740 --> 00:39:34.470
And so one of the things that
we want the internal preview

00:39:34.470 --> 00:39:39.030
to enable you to do
is to try that out.

00:39:39.030 --> 00:39:41.790
We would like you to be able
to say, in your global JSON

00:39:41.790 --> 00:39:46.470
or at the command line, hey,
give me the 11 nightlies.

00:39:46.470 --> 00:39:50.790
Give me the hot builds for 11,
and just let me have a way

00:39:50.790 --> 00:39:53.880
to try those out easily.

00:39:53.880 --> 00:39:55.290
But we also want
to give you a way

00:39:55.290 --> 00:39:58.740
to easily A-B compare behaviors.

00:39:58.740 --> 00:40:01.500
And so I call this
one-shot execution.

00:40:01.500 --> 00:40:04.760
We want there to be a
command called dotnetup.NET

00:40:04.760 --> 00:40:09.290
that you can use to specify
a specific version of.NET

00:40:09.290 --> 00:40:11.540
to run a given command on.

00:40:11.540 --> 00:40:15.260
So, in your case, it may be
as simple as "dotnet build."

00:40:15.260 --> 00:40:19.560
But you want to validate
that.NET build on 10.0.300.

00:40:19.560 --> 00:40:24.480
Works the same for you that.NET
build on 11, Preview 5 does.

00:40:24.480 --> 00:40:28.020
With this one-shot execution,
you could specifically say, hey,

00:40:28.020 --> 00:40:32.300
dotnetup, run.NET
build on 10.0.300,

00:40:32.300 --> 00:40:37.320
and then run.NET build on 11,
Preview 5, and you could compare

00:40:37.320 --> 00:40:39.450
and contrast those experiences.

00:40:39.450 --> 00:40:41.310
Maybe there's a UX
change to the way

00:40:41.310 --> 00:40:43.410
that the MSBuild Terminal
Logger shows things

00:40:43.410 --> 00:40:45.360
that you need to react to.

00:40:45.360 --> 00:40:49.590
Or maybe there's a behavioral
change in the SDK targets.

00:40:49.590 --> 00:40:52.740
Or maybe there's just a CLI tool
that is tweaked that you want

00:40:52.740 --> 00:40:55.020
to try out a bug fix on.

00:40:55.020 --> 00:40:58.860
One-shot execution should make
it easier for you to A-B test

00:40:58.860 --> 00:41:03.390
and do so in a way that doesn't
impact your global installation.

00:41:03.390 --> 00:41:07.380
So I'm very excited for that,
for people to try ad hoc fixes

00:41:07.380 --> 00:41:09.960
and give us even
faster feedback.

00:41:09.960 --> 00:41:13.380
So all of that is internal
preview: daily builds,

00:41:13.380 --> 00:41:16.290
one-shot execution, stable SDKs.

00:41:16.290 --> 00:41:19.290
The next milestone that we're
aiming for is public preview.

00:41:19.290 --> 00:41:20.940
In this milestone, it's more

00:41:20.940 --> 00:41:24.740
about helping you
stay compliant.

00:41:24.740 --> 00:41:27.470
The public preview
will have the ability

00:41:27.470 --> 00:41:31.250
for dotnetup itself
to update itself.

00:41:31.250 --> 00:41:34.100
Those of you who have used the
Aspire CLI have seen how useful

00:41:34.100 --> 00:41:36.360
this can be in practice.

00:41:36.360 --> 00:41:38.520
But dotnetup, once
installed, should be able

00:41:38.520 --> 00:41:41.730
to just take control of its
own updating from there,

00:41:41.730 --> 00:41:45.030
with all appropriate signing
and validation there.

00:41:45.030 --> 00:41:47.490
In addition, we want
dotnetup to be able

00:41:47.490 --> 00:41:52.630
to give you update checks
and notifications as well.

00:41:53.970 --> 00:41:57.540
Those of you who have used tools
like Oh My Posh or Oh My Zsh,

00:41:57.540 --> 00:42:00.480
when you start up a terminal,
when you're using these tools,

00:42:00.480 --> 00:42:04.020
once a day or once a week,
they do a check to see

00:42:04.020 --> 00:42:06.720
if there's a new version
and then let you know.

00:42:06.720 --> 00:42:09.450
We want to do the same
both for dotnetup as well

00:42:09.450 --> 00:42:12.750
as your installed
SDKs so that you know

00:42:12.750 --> 00:42:15.390
when there are security
updates or feature updates,

00:42:15.390 --> 00:42:18.360
and you can hop on board those
trains and take advantage

00:42:18.360 --> 00:42:20.760
of them as soon as possible.

00:42:20.760 --> 00:42:23.610
Public preview will also have
full signature verification

00:42:23.610 --> 00:42:28.800
of all stable releases, both
the.NET SDK and runtime tooling,

00:42:28.800 --> 00:42:31.290
as well as dotnetup
itself, again,

00:42:31.290 --> 00:42:34.310
for that provenance stuff
I mentioned earlier on.

00:42:34.310 --> 00:42:36.680
When you use this tool, we
want you to be confident

00:42:36.680 --> 00:42:39.230
that you are using the bits

00:42:39.230 --> 00:42:43.100
that Microsoft intended
for you to use here.

00:42:43.100 --> 00:42:44.480
It's about safety, security,

00:42:44.480 --> 00:42:47.130
and managing your supply
chain in that way.

00:42:47.130 --> 00:42:48.600
For public preview,
I'd also like us

00:42:48.600 --> 00:42:51.390
to have agent skills
available and published

00:42:51.390 --> 00:42:54.960
to the dotnet/skills repo
so that those of you

00:42:54.960 --> 00:42:59.310
who are using LLMs to
do automated updates

00:42:59.310 --> 00:43:04.740
or do A-B testing, compare and
contrast between versions,

00:43:04.740 --> 00:43:08.670
can have good ways of using
dotnetup easily without having

00:43:08.670 --> 00:43:11.730
to write a bunch of skills
in context yourself.

00:43:11.730 --> 00:43:15.690
For GA, we want to focus on fit
and finish of the tool itself,

00:43:15.690 --> 00:43:19.920
official docs up on the.NET
website, mentioning

00:43:19.920 --> 00:43:23.880
and supporting dotnetup as
an official install method

00:43:23.880 --> 00:43:28.250
on the.NET website itself,
and also expanding the usage

00:43:28.250 --> 00:43:31.070
of global JSON to
manage those runtimes,

00:43:31.070 --> 00:43:34.650
that hypothetical
I showed earlier.

00:43:35.960 --> 00:43:40.620
In the far future, dotnetup,
I hope, is a vehicle for us

00:43:40.620 --> 00:43:44.310
to radically change the
way we ship the SDK

00:43:44.310 --> 00:43:48.690
and enable faster updates
of all of our tooling.

00:43:48.690 --> 00:43:51.210
I don't know if you've
noticed, but with AI

00:43:51.210 --> 00:43:54.510
and LLMs exploding the way
they have, there's a push

00:43:54.510 --> 00:43:59.130
to release earlier and more
often and more incrementally.

00:43:59.130 --> 00:44:06.180
The way you manage risk is by
broad testing and faster rollout

00:44:06.180 --> 00:44:09.450
so that if there is a problem,
you can react to it faster.

00:44:09.450 --> 00:44:11.550
We want that for
not just dotnetup,

00:44:11.550 --> 00:44:13.830
but the entire.NET toolchain.

00:44:13.830 --> 00:44:15.600
But it can be hard
to do with some

00:44:15.600 --> 00:44:18.660
of these delivery mechanisms
that I've talked about earlier.

00:44:18.660 --> 00:44:23.070
We think dotnetup can help us
accelerate our delivery as well

00:44:23.070 --> 00:44:26.970
as help factor out the SDK
itself into components

00:44:26.970 --> 00:44:30.720
that may be able to
update more rapidly

00:44:30.720 --> 00:44:32.940
through those
traditional channels.

00:44:32.940 --> 00:44:36.030
We also want to focus longer
term on integrating dotnetup

00:44:36.030 --> 00:44:38.340
into the existing
CI/CD integrations

00:44:38.340 --> 00:44:41.610
that you use to
manage.NET today.

00:44:41.610 --> 00:44:43.500
Action/setup.net
for GitHub Actions,

00:44:43.500 --> 00:44:47.970
and the usedotnet 2 action on
Azure DevOps are great tools

00:44:47.970 --> 00:44:51.930
to have, but their features
subtly differ sometimes.

00:44:51.930 --> 00:44:54.780
And they may not have all
of the same capabilities.

00:44:54.780 --> 00:44:57.900
They may not be, necessarily,
investing in the same amount

00:44:57.900 --> 00:44:59.760
of caching or performance.

00:44:59.760 --> 00:45:05.250
We are hopeful that by using
dotnetup as the implementation

00:45:05.250 --> 00:45:09.120
of those tools, that we can
bring consistency of experience

00:45:09.120 --> 00:45:11.820
and a single place to
improve performance

00:45:11.820 --> 00:45:15.870
across environments
there as well.

00:45:15.870 --> 00:45:18.270
So that's a brief glimpse
into the roadmap.

00:45:18.270 --> 00:45:20.790
Timeline-wise, we hope to
have an internal preview

00:45:20.790 --> 00:45:24.810
in the next couple weeks and
public preview before the end

00:45:24.810 --> 00:45:26.370
of the summer, ideally.

00:45:26.370 --> 00:45:29.880
This tool, dotnetup,
is not locked

00:45:29.880 --> 00:45:33.510
to the.NET SDK ship
cycle or timeframe.

00:45:33.510 --> 00:45:36.090
It ships what we
call out of band.

00:45:36.090 --> 00:45:39.690
And so with it, all of these
gates are going to be based both

00:45:39.690 --> 00:45:43.050
on feature functionality as well
as feedback that you give us

00:45:43.050 --> 00:45:45.420
about its quality
and usefulness.

00:45:45.420 --> 00:45:48.930
So the more feedback you can
give us, trying this stuff out

00:45:48.930 --> 00:45:50.190
and kicking the tires, even

00:45:50.190 --> 00:45:53.460
on these internal nightly
bits, the better.

00:45:53.460 --> 00:45:57.240
Which leads us into what
I'd like you to do now

00:45:57.240 --> 00:46:00.630
that you've sat through
this whole talk with me.

00:46:00.630 --> 00:46:04.260
The first thing I'd like you to
do is to get dotnetup itself.

00:46:04.260 --> 00:46:06.600
Here are those aka.ms
links that you can use

00:46:06.600 --> 00:46:08.250
to easily get started.

00:46:08.250 --> 00:46:10.410
I'd start on the docs link here.

00:46:10.410 --> 00:46:14.850
So aka.ms/dotnetup/docs, that
is a Getting Started doc

00:46:14.850 --> 00:46:19.800
that will give you the exact
copy-paste shell script snippet

00:46:19.800 --> 00:46:21.480
that you can use.

00:46:21.480 --> 00:46:24.900
For the shell script, it's
exactly what I did earlier,

00:46:24.900 --> 00:46:27.450
curling the script and
piping it into SH.

00:46:27.450 --> 00:46:30.030
For the PowerShell one, it's
the PowerShell equivalent,

00:46:30.030 --> 00:46:33.870
invoke web request into
invoke expression.

00:46:33.870 --> 00:46:35.820
That's all you need
to get started there.

00:46:35.820 --> 00:46:38.010
For those of you who want to
dive a little bit deeper,

00:46:38.010 --> 00:46:40.650
there's a link to the
dotnetup design spec

00:46:40.650 --> 00:46:44.010
that we created several months
ago and have been iterating

00:46:44.010 --> 00:46:46.560
on as we add new features.

00:46:46.560 --> 00:46:50.400
If you have feedback about the
tool, the appropriate place

00:46:50.400 --> 00:46:53.730
for it is at this link,
dotnetup/feedback.

00:46:53.730 --> 00:46:55.740
That will take you to
the discussion board

00:46:55.740 --> 00:46:58.770
on the.NET SDK repo in
a special category just

00:46:58.770 --> 00:47:00.360
for dotnetup that I and the rest

00:47:00.360 --> 00:47:04.980
of the team will be
interacting with on.

00:47:04.980 --> 00:47:07.320
And then finally, all of
the samples that I showed

00:47:07.320 --> 00:47:10.500
in the demo today, as
well as GitHub actions

00:47:10.500 --> 00:47:13.590
that automatically hook up
dotnetup and do the things

00:47:13.590 --> 00:47:18.270
that I was doing today, can be
found at the sample repo I made:

00:47:18.270 --> 00:47:22.500
github.com/baronfell/
dotnetup-repo-patterns-demo.

00:47:22.500 --> 00:47:25.170
So everything I did,
you can clone this repo

00:47:25.170 --> 00:47:27.720
and get started and
try it yourself.

00:47:27.720 --> 00:47:29.460
We'd love you to get the tool,

00:47:29.460 --> 00:47:32.310
try it on the samples,
try it on your repo.

00:47:32.310 --> 00:47:35.490
Let us know what
you think of it.

00:47:35.490 --> 00:47:36.450
Does it flow well?

00:47:36.450 --> 00:47:37.590
Is it performant enough?

00:47:37.590 --> 00:47:40.170
Does it have the
features that you want?

00:47:40.170 --> 00:47:41.730
Let us know at the
feedback link.

00:47:41.730 --> 00:47:44.070
And thank you so
much for watching.

00:47:44.070 --> 00:47:47.950
Have a great rest of the time
at Build, and happy hacking.
